.TH dcheck "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
dcheck \- directory consistency check
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdcheck [\-s] [\-i \fIinumber ...\fB] \fIfilesystem ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
dcheck -- directory consistency check
Usage:	dcheck [ -s ] [ -i inumber ... ] filesystem ...
.HE
.B dcheck
checks the consistency of the directory structure of each named
.IR filesystem .
It makes two passes over the i-list: the first walks every directory
reachable from the root and counts, for every i-number, how many
directory entries name it; the second compares that count against the
link count recorded in the i-node itself.
.PP
For every i-node whose entry count does not match its link count,
.B dcheck
prints a line giving the i-number, the entry count, and the link
count (marked
.B (u)
if the i-node is otherwise unused, i.e. its mode is zero); if
.B \-s
was given, it corrects the on-disc link count to match instead of
printing anything.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Instead of reporting bad link counts, repair them: an i-node with a
nonzero entry count has its link count set to that entry count; an
i-node with a zero entry count and a nonzero mode is cleared
(equivalent to
.BR clri );
an allocated i-node (nonzero mode) whose entry count reaches zero only
after some directory entries pointing to it are themselves cleared
during the second pass requires a further, third pass, which
.B dcheck
runs automatically when needed.
This option requires write access to the raw
.IR filesystem .
.IP "\fB\-i \fIinumber ...\fR"
While walking the directories, also report every entry found whose
i-number appears in this list, printing the i-number, the containing
directory's i-number, and the entry name, tagged
.BR arg .
Up to 20 i-numbers may be given.
.PP
Any directory entry whose i-number exceeds the highest valid i-number
for the file system is reported as
.B bad
regardless of the
.B \-i
list.
.PP
Because
.B dcheck
makes more than one pass over the file system, the
.I filesystem
should be unmounted (or mounted read-only) while it runs; use the raw
device.
If
.B \-s
is used on the root file system, the system should be rebooted
immediately afterward, without an intervening
.BR sync .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR check ,
.BR icheck ,
.BR sync ,
.B umount
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B dcheck
exits with a status that is the logical OR of small flag values
recorded in
.BR check.h :
1 for a usage or open error, 2 for an unrecoverable read or write
error, 4 if an i-node with a zero link count was found (and
.B \-s
was not given), and 8 if an i-node's link count needed correcting (and
.B \-s
was not given).
An exit status of zero means no inconsistency was found.
.SH NOTES
.B dcheck
requires a
.I filesystem
argument; it has no notion of a default file system to check.
